From 9144648e59c3624843c6d2baf8b6ef65f9b39555 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: y-o-o-z <136268860+y-o-o-z@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 15:28:59 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] local: fe-web password in a header, safer pin, sessions that
 expire

fe-web password: it went in the WebSocket URL (?password=...), and the URL
was written to the debug log. erssi now takes "Authorization: Bearer
<password>" on the upgrade request (and after 5 failures in a minute
checks one login every 2 s), so the password goes only in that header, as UTF-8
bytes, and the URL no longer carries it. The settings handlers
(irssi:config:save, irssi:config:test) logged the whole request, plaintext
fe-web password included; the password is now masked there.

Certificate pin: the connection test in the settings connected without
the pin (any certificate), handing the typed password to whatever answered
on the port. The pin logic now lives in one helper (feWebPin.ts) used by
the real connection and the test. It is also stricter: a caFile the user
set that cannot be read stops the connection (with an error in the log)
instead of silently trusting any certificate; the default
~/.erssi/fe-web-cert.pem is used only for an erssi on this machine
(127.0.0.1, ::1, localhost), as a remote erssi's certificate is not ours;
and the certificate is read again on every (re)connect, so one that erssi
writes after Nexus started, or regenerates, is picked up. A local erssi
without the file yet (first run) still connects unpinned, with a warning.

Sign-out: the browser emitted "sign-out" and reloaded at once, so the
request could die with the page and the token keep working. It now waits
for the server's acknowledgement (sent once the session is removed from
the user file), at most 3 s.

Session expiry: the 30-day expiry ran only when Nexus started, and
lastUse changed only at sign-in, so a session used every day through one
long-open tab could expire right after it closed, and old ones lived on
while Nexus ran. lastUse is now updated when a browser leaves, and the
expiry runs daily and at every sign-in.

Sign-in races: a token sign-in updated its session with
_.assign(sessions[token] || {}, ...), which brought back a session signed
out between the token check and that point; a token sign-in now only
updates an existing session and fails (auth:failed) when it is gone. A
browser that disconnected while its new token was being made still got a
session and a browserTokens entry that nothing removed; it no longer does.

Password change: the other sessions stayed signed in after the Nexus
password changed; they are signed out now (as "sign out other sessions").

erssi journal: a late journal start (networks that connected after Nexus)
kept going after the fe-web connection dropped or a new init set the
journal up again while it loaded history, and could put a stale "erssi"
window group back. It now stops when superseded, and does not clear the
"setup running" mark of the setup that superseded it.

server_status "disconnected" for a server that never registered (a failed
/connect) created and announced an empty network; it is ignored now.

The journal directory was one for the whole process (ERSSI_JOURNAL_DIR or
~/.erssi/journal), so every user, whatever erssi they connect to, would
see the history of the erssi on this machine. irssiConnection.journalDir
sets it per user; without it the default is used only when the user's
erssi is on this machine, otherwise there is no journal.
